<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:ext="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog-ext"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd
                        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog-ext http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog-ext.xsd">

    <!--
        Added the entity Shift: Phase 4's P4.1, and the roster gate.

        WHY IT EXISTS
        The client's ruling 1a makes the roster the single source of truth for "who is on duty". Today that question
        is answered by ward_cover, which says which doctor covers which ward for a period - a second writable table
        stating its own answer. Two tables that both claim to say who is on duty always diverge, and the divergence
        is found during an incident. This table becomes the answer; ward_cover becomes read-only for one release and
        its rows become shifts.

        WHY staff_record_id AND NOT user_id
        A shift belongs to a person, not to an account. The roster has to hold the people who never sign in - a
        cleaner, a porter, a driver - and a member of staff is a person's employment while an account is their
        credentials. Rostering the account would leave everyone without one unrostered, and a ward that cannot see
        the porter on duty is a ward that cannot find them. The link from staff_record to a login stays optional and
        points the other way.

        WHY shift_date IS A DATE SEPARATE FROM THE TIMES
        The roster is read as a calendar: "who is on Thursday" must have one answer whatever the clock says. A night
        shift that runs past midnight still belongs to the day it started, which is why the day is a column of its
        own rather than being derived from starts_at.

        WHY ward_id IS NULLABLE
        A shift can be on no particular ward - a porter's, or a matron's across the hospital. Refusing that would
        force a placeholder ward into the data, and the roster would carry that lie for as long as it existed.

        WHY (staff_record_id, shift_date) IS UNIQUE
        One person, one shift a day - the phase document's own wording, and what makes "who is on Thursday" a single
        answer. The consequence to keep in mind: it also refuses a genuine double shift (a 12-hour day followed by a
        12-hour night on the same date). If that is real at this hospital, the index becomes
        (staff_record_id, shift_date, shift_type) in one migration - which is why it is written down here rather than
        left for someone to discover from a failed insert.

        WHY THERE IS NO SAMPLE DATA
        Same reason as staff_record: invented people on invented shifts are fabricated employment records, and a
        development database has a way of becoming a real one. A fresh database starts with an empty roster.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20261006100000-1" author="hms">
        <comment>Add the shift table: who is on duty, when, and where</comment>
        <createTable tableName="shift">
            <column name="id" type="bigint">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="shift_date" type="date" remarks="The day the roster reads; a shift past midnight belongs to the day it started">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
            <column name="shift_type" type="varchar(20)" remarks="DAY or NIGHT today; stored as a string, so a value added later needs no migration">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
            <column name="starts_at" type="time">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
            <column name="ends_at" type="time">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
            <column name="note" type="varchar(500)">
                <constraints nullable="true" />
            </column>
            <column name="staff_record_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
            <column name="ward_id" type="bigint" remarks="Optional: a porter or a matron is on duty with no particular ward">
                <constraints nullable="true" />
            </column>
            <column name="created_by_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false" />
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint
            columnNames="staff_record_id, shift_date"
            constraintName="ux_shift__staff_record__shift_date"
            tableName="shift"/>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
